yword","text":"rethrows"},{"kind":"text","text":" -> ["},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Element","preciseIdentifier":"s:ST7ElementQa"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"}],"role":"symbol","title":"reductions(_:)","abstract":[],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques\/reductions(_:)","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques":{"url":"\/documentation\/cliques","kind":"symbol","title":"Cliques","type":"topic","role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques","abstract":[]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/MaximalCliques":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques","title":"MaximalCliques","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every maximal clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph once, lazily, each in "},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering"},{"text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"degeneracy d.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}]}}}